# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for provizio_dds sanitizer builds (applied via TSAN_OPTIONS).
# Deadlock detection stays ENABLED — only the specific false positives below are suppressed.
#
# --- Data races ---
# Only symbol-specific entries, for races that are genuinely Fast-DDS-internal: any pattern broad
# enough to cover Fast-DDS internals would also silence a real race in provizio_dds's own listener
# callbacks (they run on Fast-DDS-owned threads, so their stacks always contain Fast-DDS frames).
# Never a namespace- or library-wide pattern.
#
# Fast-DDS 3.6.2: StatefulWriter::intraprocess_heartbeat (the ReaderProxy's initial heartbeat,
# on the timer thread) reads ReaderLocator::local_reader_ without the writer mutex, while
# ReaderLocator::stop() resets it under that mutex from matched_reader_remove on a receive
# thread when the remote participant goes away -- the header documents the member as always
# protected by the writer's mutex, and the heartbeat is the one path that does not hold it.
# Intraprocess only (same-process publisher and subscriber), seen from
# discovered_endpoints_reset_does_not_spin tearing participants down. No provizio_dds frame on
# either stack. Fixed upstream by eProsima/Fast-DDS#6422 (milestone v3.6.3.0), which takes the
# mutex around that read: drop this entry when FAST_DDS_VERSION moves past 3.6.2.
race:eprosima::fastdds::rtps::StatefulWriter::intraprocess_heartbeat
#
# --- Lock-order inversions (potential deadlocks) ---
# provizio_dds holds its participant lifecycle lock `reset_mutex` in SHARED (read) mode across calls
# into Fast-DDS, and Fast-DDS discovery/listener threads holding their own locks invoke provizio_dds
# callbacks that read-lock `reset_mutex` (via these accessors). TSan's deadlock detector reports the
# resulting lock-order cycles, but they CANNOT deadlock: shared read-locks are mutually compatible, and
# TSan simply does not model std::shared_mutex read mode. Suppress at the read-lock acquisition sites
# only. The EXCLUSIVE acquisitions — the network-recovery reset and the deferred-reader swap
# (domain_participant.cpp, `std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex>`) — are deliberately NOT listed, so a
# genuine reset-vs-* deadlock, a data race, or a deadlock among provizio_dds's other mutexes is still
# reported. Add any NEW reset_mutex shared-lock accessor here (get_guid / get_num_matched_publishers /
# publish etc. acquire it via fastdds_participant and are already covered).
deadlock:provizio::dds::domain_participant::fastdds_participant
deadlock:provizio::dds::domain_participant::register_endpoint
deadlock:provizio::dds::domain_participant::register_type
deadlock:provizio::dds::domain_participant::register_topic
deadlock:provizio::dds::domain_participant::on_discovered_endpoint
